examples.question_text:
Decide whether each pair of lines is parallel.
(a) $y=\frac{1}{2}x+2$ and $y=\frac{1}{2}x-1$.
(b) $y=7-3x$ and $y=3x+7$.
The diagram shows the lines in part (a).
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=8mm,y=8mm,>=stealth,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{lineBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{lineRed}{HTML}{EF6068}
\draw[black,->] (-3,0)--(4,0);
\draw[black,->] (0,-3)--(0,4.5);
\path (4,0) node[below] {$x$} (0,4.5) node[left] {$y$};
\draw[lineBlue,thick,<->] (-3,0.5)--(4,4);
\draw[lineRed,thick,dashed,<->] (-3,-2.5)--(4,1);
\path (-2,2.5) node {$y=\frac12x+2$} (2,-1.5) node {$y=\frac12x-1$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
(a) Both gradients are $\frac{1}{2}$. The intercepts are different, so the lines are parallel.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=8mm,y=8mm,>=stealth,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{lineBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{lineRed}{HTML}{EF6068}
\definecolor{guideGreen}{HTML}{4F9B63}
\draw[black,->] (-3,0)--(4,0);
\draw[black,->] (0,-3)--(0,4.5);
\path (4,0) node[below] {$x$} (0,4.5) node[left] {$y$};
\draw[lineBlue,thick,<->] (-3,0.5)--(4,4);
\draw[lineRed,thick,dashed,<->] (-3,-2.5)--(4,1);
\draw[guideGreen,dotted,thick] (0,2)--(2,2)--(2,3);
\draw[guideGreen,dotted,thick] (0,-1)--(2,-1)--(2,0);
\path (1,2) node[below] {$2$} (2,2.5) node[right] {$1$} (1,-1) node[below] {$2$} (2,-0.5) node[right] {$1$};
\path (-2,2.5) node {$m=\frac12$} (-2,-2.5) node[below] {$m=\frac12$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
(b) The gradients are $-3$ and $3$. They are different, so the lines are not parallel.
